---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import GlossaryLink from '../../components/GlossaryLink.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Email Triage"
  description="The email-triage skill pulls your Outlook inbox every morning, sorts it into four quadrants using YOUR rules, and hands you a one-pager of only what actually needs you — with draft replies ready to go."
  pageId="email-triage"
  readMinutes={6}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="📬"
    title="Email Triage — your inbox, sorted every morning"
    subtitle="Most of your email doesn't need you. This skill finds the handful of messages that do — and drafts the replies."
    readMinutes={6}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      Say <strong>"triage my inbox"</strong> to Claude. The first time, it asks you six quick questions
      to learn your job. Every time after, it reads your recent Outlook email and hands you a
      one-page report: what to <strong>do</strong>, what to <strong>schedule</strong>, what to
      <strong>delegate</strong>, and how much it safely <strong>dropped</strong> — with draft replies
      written for the items that genuinely need you. It never sends anything. Ever.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      This skill started life as Richard Kilshaw's personal morning routine — a codified filter for
      deciding which emails were actually <em>his</em> problem. It worked so well we generalized it
      for everyone. The core insight survives: you are not the owner of most email in your inbox.
      Someone else owns it, a machine sent it, or nobody needs it. The skill's whole job is to be
      ruthless about that on your behalf.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- The four quadrants -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">🗂️ The four quadrants</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted">Every email lands in exactly one bucket — a classic called the Eisenhower matrix.</p>

    <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 mb-5">
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-gold">Q1 · DO</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Actions only <em>you</em> can take — an approval with your name on it, a direct question nobody else is handling. Each one comes with a link to the email and a <strong>draft reply ready to copy</strong>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-chat">Q2 · SCHEDULE</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Important awareness you don't own — results in someone else's area, a deck to read, admin that can wait. Skim it when you choose to, not when it arrives.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-cowork">Q3 · DELEGATE</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Urgent, but owned by someone else. The report names the owner from <em>your</em> delegation map, so forwarding takes ten seconds.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-dim">Q4 · DROP</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Automated notifications, tracking emails, out-of-office replies, newsletters. You get a <em>count</em>, not a list — because you were never going to read them anyway.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Callout variant="gold" title="An empty DO list is a win">
      <p>
        The skill is deliberately strict: when it's unsure whether something is truly yours, it files
        it under awareness rather than action. Some mornings the report says
        <em>"0 of 25 emails need you."</em> That's not a failure — that's the whole point.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <!-- First run -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">🎬 Your first run: six questions</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted">The skill doesn't guess what matters to you — it asks. Once. Your answers become a personal rules file it uses every morning after.</p>

    <div class="prose-content">
      <ol>
        <li><strong>What's your role — what decisions can only you make?</strong> Be honest about your actual function, not your title. "I approve purchase requests over $500" beats "I'm a manager."</li>
        <li><strong>Which email types always need action from you?</strong> Approvals, access requests, direct questions, data handoffs.</li>
        <li><strong>Who do you delegate to, and what do they own?</strong> This becomes the map that powers the DELEGATE quadrant.</li>
        <li><strong>What never needs to be seen individually?</strong> Jira pings, HR reminders, shipping trackers — your personal noise list.</li>
        <li><strong>Where should your rules live?</strong> So they persist between sessions.</li>
        <li><strong>Want it to run automatically every weekday morning — and at what time?</strong> Optional, and only offered where your setup supports it (see below).</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The better your answers, the sharper the triage — and you can refine them forever. Whenever a
        classification is wrong, just say so and the skill updates your rules on the spot:
      </p>
    </div>
    <CopyPrompt text="That vendor quote email is actually mine to handle — quotes for IT equipment always come to me. Update my triage rules." />
  </section>

  <!-- Scheduling -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">⏰ Making it automatic</h2>
    <div class="prose-content">
      <p>
        In the <strong>Claude Desktop app</strong> (including <GlossaryLink term="cowork">Co-Work</GlossaryLink>),
        question six can set up a scheduled task that runs the triage every weekday at a time you pick —
        so the report is waiting with your coffee.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Callout variant="cowork" title="Two things to know about scheduled runs">
      <p>
        <strong>1. Your computer must be on</strong> with the Claude app open at the scheduled time — if
        it isn't, the triage simply runs the next time you open the app, so you never miss a morning
        entirely. <strong>2. Click "Run now" once</strong> (Scheduled section, sidebar) right after
        setup — the permissions you approve on that first run carry forward, so future mornings never
        stall waiting for you to click "allow."
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <div class="prose-content">
      <p>
        Using Claude in the <strong>browser (chat)</strong> instead? Scheduled tasks aren't available
        there, but the skill works fine on demand — your rules live in Claude's
        <a href={href('/memory/')} class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">memory</a> instead of
        a file, and the report arrives right in the conversation. Just make "triage my inbox" the first
        thing you type each morning.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Safety -->
  <section class="mb-10 reveal">
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">🛡️ What it will never do</h2>
    <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 mb-5">
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Read-only, always</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">It never sends, replies, forwards, archives, or deletes anything. Draft replies live in the report for <em>you</em> to copy, edit, and send. Your inbox is untouched.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hover-lift rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-4">
        <p class="mb-1 font-semibold text-text">Built-in fraud guard</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Emails asking you to pay a bill, change a PO, or grant access get extra scrutiny. If anything smells off — a mismatched sender, artificial urgency — the skill flags it for out-of-band verification instead of drafting a compliant reply. Fake payment requests are the most common way companies get robbed by email.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Callout variant="gold" title="✅ And the usual reassurance">
      <p>
        We're on <strong>Claude Enterprise</strong>: your email and your rules are never used to train
        the model. Details on the <a href={href('/safety/')} class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">Safety &amp; Data</a> page.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="🎛️ Which model? Sonnet, medium effort">
    <p>
      Your inbox doesn't need the biggest brain — the skill's judgment lives in <em>your rules file</em>,
      so <a href={href('/models/')} class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">Sonnet</a> at
      <a href={href('/effort/')} class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">medium effort</a> gives the
      same triage quality as Opus at a fraction of the token spend. Set both next to the model picker
      before your first run — scheduled runs inherit whatever the task was created with.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout variant="chat" title="📋 Before your first run: one requirement">
    <p>
      The skill reads your inbox through the <strong>Microsoft 365 connector</strong>. Check that it's
      enabled: Claude → Settings → Connectors → Microsoft 365. If it's missing or you hit a wall, email
      <strong><a href="mailto:jake@aptera.us" class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">jake@aptera.us</a></strong>.
      More on connectors on the <a href={href('/connectors/')} class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2">Connectors &amp; Skills</a> page.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <TryItNow title="Try it right now">
    <p>Open Claude — chat, Desktop, or Co-Work — and say:</p>
    <CopyPrompt text="Triage my inbox." />
    <p>
      That's it. If it's your first time, budget five minutes for the six setup questions — then watch
      it cut a day's worth of email down to the two that matter. It reads everything from the past
      24 hours (72 on Mondays), and if you're back from vacation, just say how far to go:
      <em>"triage my inbox — I've been out since the 10th."</em>
    </p>
  </TryItNow>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>Say <strong>"triage my inbox"</strong> — the skill sorts your Outlook email into <strong>Do / Schedule / Delegate / Drop</strong> using rules built from <em>your</em> answers.</li>
      <li>Q1 items come with <strong>draft replies ready to copy</strong>; Q4 noise is counted, not shown.</li>
      <li>It's <strong>read-only</strong> — it never sends or touches your email — and payment or access requests that look suspicious get flagged, not drafted.</li>
      <li>On the Desktop app it can <strong>run automatically every weekday morning</strong> (computer on, app open); in browser chat, just ask each morning.</li>
      <li>Wrong call? Tell it — <em>"that one's actually mine"</em> — and your rules improve for every morning after.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
